Parallel Tight JPEG compression

JPEG subrectangles don't depend on any encoder state (other Tight
subencodings use persistent zlib streams), so while a large rectangle is
being encoded, they are queued and compressed by worker threads instead,
and sent as soon as they're done. Solid, mono and indexed subrectangles are
still encoded in order by the client thread. Rectangles of an update can be
sent in any order, so the encoded stream stays valid.

